        "Foundations and History: From Vote Counting to Effect Size Synthesis",
        "Scrub the chapter's foundations: define meta-analysis as the quantitative synthesis of results across multiple studies, sort its history from vote counting through Smith and Glass (1977) to the modern weighted models, distinguish meta-analysis from systematic review and narrative review, and identify the four core affordances of mapping, summarizing, comparing, and assessing bias. Then defend the method against the classic complaints of 'mega-silliness,' 'apples and oranges,' and 'garbage in, garbage out,' and decide when a research question is focused enough to be meta-analyzed.",
